<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Anglo-Dutch War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1665">1665</a>–<a href="/years/1667">1667</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Dutch War</a> was a naval and colonial war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, fought from 1665 to 1667 over trade and overseas possessions. After English gains such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> in 1664, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> won the Four Days' Battle, raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a> in 1667 and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> of 1667 left each side with what it held.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war.jpg" alt="Second Anglo-Dutch War" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abraham Storck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Storck,_Four_Days_Battle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Second Anglo-Dutch War began on 4 March 1665 and ended with the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> on 31 July 1667. It followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-dutch-war">First Anglo-Dutch War</a> of 1652 to 1654, whose treaty had not settled the issues behind it, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navigation-acts">Navigation Acts</a> and English objections to Dutch trading monopolies. Charles II renewed the Acts in August 1660 and strengthened them in 1663.</p><p>In 1660 he and his brother James founded the Royal African Company to challenge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> dominance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. In August 1664 the English occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, and took Dutch slaving posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michiel-de-ruyter">Michiel de Ruyter</a> recaptured in early 1665. After English attacks on convoys, the Dutch declared war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At the Battle of Lowestoft on 13 June 1665 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> lost some sixteen ships and over 30 per cent of their personnel, among them their commander Jacob van Wassenaer. De Ruyter then became commander in chief. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 16 January 1666 and Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> followed in February. The Four Days' Battle, fought from 11 to 15 June 1666, cost the English ten ships and the Dutch four. The English won the St. James's Day Battle of 4 and 5 August, but on 19 August up to 150 Dutch merchantmen were destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlie">Vlie</a> estuary in Holmes's Bonfire.</p><p>English finances then collapsed. The Great Plague of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and the Fire of London in September 1666 turned opinion against the war. By early 1667 much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> lay laid up at Chatham, and peace talks opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> in March. In June De Ruyter led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a>, broke the chain guarding it and attacked the fleet on 13 June. Three major warships were burnt and HMS Royal Charles was taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. In the Caribbean Abraham Crijnssen captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> in February 1667.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under the Treaty of Breda England kept New Netherland, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> kept Pulau Run, Fort Cormantin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, Sint Eustatius and their West African posts. The Navigation Act was modified in the Dutch favour, and England accepted the principle of Free Ship, Free Goods. The Dutch regarded the peace as a personal triumph for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-de-witt">Johan de Witt</a>. Fear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and its invasion of the Spanish Netherlands made both sides prefer a quick end, and in 1668 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1668">Triple Alliance</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, England and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Charles II's wish to avenge the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a> setback led to the Third Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Dutch War</a> in 1672.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Anglo-Dutch War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Breda of 1667 ended the <b>Second Anglo-Dutch War</b>; the Dutch did not press their claim, keeping Suriname and the nutmeg island of Run instead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Second Anglo-Dutch War</b> a fleet from the Dutch province of Zeeland under Abraham Crijnssen captured its main fort in February 1667 and renamed it Fort Zeelandia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1667, during the <b>Second Anglo-Dutch War</b>, a Zeeland fleet under Abraham Crijnssen captured the colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/willoughbyland">Willoughbyland</a></li></ul></section>
